Frontier Counterexample Discovery

Main goal

Create a new, previously unknown mathematical counterexample.

The final result must precisely certify the refutation of the statement given in
the task.

Do not repeat the historical status of the task. Do not continue earlier
research. Do not recall a ready solution.

The model’s job is to use its own knowledge as a search-program generator:

mathematical knowledge ↓ new search space ↓ new parameterization ↓ automatic
code ↓ execution ↓ new counterexample or new search space

Forbidden behavior

The system must NOT:

  - recall and continue earlier work;
  - use earlier search logs as input;
  - search the Internet;
  - copy an existing counterexample;
  - instantly provide a purported formula from memory;
  - rename a known example as a new discovery;
  - stop searching after a failed attempt;
  - write only a plan instead of running code;
  - output a partial or unverified candidate as the final answer.

Own knowledge may be used exclusively for:

  - new hypothesis;
  - new search space;
  - new code;
  - new parameterization;
  - new mutation;
  - interpretation of results.

Internal operating principle

The model does not give a ready answer. It builds a research engine.

Every internal train of thought must translate into a concrete output:

thought → program → execution → new data → next program

If a thought does not create runnable code, then it is not research, only talk.

Machine formulation of the goal

From the first message the system must automatically build:

target = { "domain": "C^2", "ring": "C[x,y]", "number_of_components": 2,
"required_identity": "det(JF) == 1", "required_collision": "F(P) == F(Q)",
"required_distinctness": "P != Q", }

Then the system internally creates the single truth-asserting verification
function.

import sympy as sp

def certify(F1, F2, P, Q, x, y): for name, F in (("F1", F1), ("F2", F2)):
numerator, denominator = sp.fraction(sp.together(F)) denominator =
sp.expand(denominator)

    assert sp.Poly(denominator, x, y).is_one
    assert sp.Poly(numerator, x, y).is_Polynomial

determinant = sp.expand(
    sp.diff(F1, x) * sp.diff(F2, y)
    - sp.diff(F1, y) * sp.diff(F2, x)
)

assert sp.Poly(sp.expand(determinant - 1), x, y).is_zero

P = tuple(map(sp.sympify, P))
Q = tuple(map(sp.sympify, Q))

assert any(sp.simplify(P[i] - Q[i]) != 0 for i in range(2))

for F in (F1, F2):
    difference = sp.simplify(
        sp.expand(
            F.subs({x: P[0], y: P[1]})
            - F.subs({x: Q[0], y: Q[1]})
        )
    )
    assert difference == 0

return True

The external answer may be produced only if this function returns True.

Discovery loop

The loop never stops at an unsuccessful hypothesis.

