A negative outcome of the loop becomes new input for extend.

Automatic search-space generation

The system must build the search frontier from scratch.

Treat every hypothesis as an independently generated family of parameters.

The generator must provide:

  - free coefficients;
  - monomial support;
  - symmetry relations;
  - possible auxiliary variables;
  - elimination equations;
  - collision conditions;
  - verification code.

The code must always generate exact equations.

Mandatory search operations

1. Monomial space search

The system must automatically generate various full and sparse monomial
supports.

Do not try a single support. Examine thousands of different combinations
simultaneously.

2. Degree raising

If no hit at one degree:

  - raise the same structure to a higher degree;
  - attach new monomial groups;
  - take into account equations ruled out at earlier levels;
  - do not run the same closed system again.

3. Parameterization

Do not search continuous coefficients without bounds. The code must generate
rational, algebraic, and symbolic parameterizations as well as heuristic ones.

4. Composition

The system must look for new variants by composing two or more lower-order
mappings.

The components and the order of composition must be searched together.

5. Hidden variable

The system must introduce new auxiliary variables and then eliminate them.

However, the final object must still reside in the original two-variable space.

6. Elimination

The code must regularly run:

  - Gröbner-basis computation;
  - resultants;
  - lexicographic elimination;
  - saturation;
  - component separation;
  - decomposition into primes;
  - Gröbner-based specialization.

7. Numerical pre-screening

Numerical search may be used to produce candidates, but is not tolerated as
certification.

Every numerical candidate must be converted back into exact symbolic form.

8. Mutation

From every result at least five new search families must be created:

  - support modification;
  - symmetry modification;
  - parameter densification;
  - compositional rearrangement;
  - change of elimination target.

Parallel discovery

The system must run several completely independent searchers at the same time.

Every searcher gets:

  - its own process;
  - its own working directory;
  - its own random seed;
  - its own search frontier;
  - a shared certifier.

Parallel searchers must not examine the same family.

If none yields a counterexample, new parameter families must be created by
crossing the frontiers.

Internal memory

The system uses only the results of the current run.

