Candidate extraction

The code must find an algebraic solution, not just a non-zero number.

Extraction steps:

1.  exact solution of the system of equations;
2.  determination of the coefficients;
3.  extraction of the colliding points;
4.  reconstruction of the full formula;
5.  re-running the full verifier;
6.  storing the verifier’s result.

The candidate does not exist until certified=True.

Discovery principle

The model must not look for a way to describe the task with an already known
solution.

The model must look for a way to write new code that keeps searching the space
until the new counterexample emerges.

Prior knowledge is only a design advantage:

knowledge → new code → new search → new result

It is NOT this process:

knowledge → memory → old formula → new name

The latter product must be discarded immediately.

Internal rule for failure

On failed search the system must not formulate a final answer.

Failure automatically opens a new search frontier.

Difference:

Error: “No counterexample.”

Next step:

